module std where

-- appendItem — append a single element to the end of a list.
-- ∀a. a → List a → List a.
--
-- Pure expression; mirrors the pre-Phase-5b4 TS native at
-- engine/src/modules/std.ts:2950 (`(item, arr) => [...toArr(arr),
-- item]`).
--
-- Param ordering `(item, arr)` deliberately matches the pre-5b4
-- TS native's argument order — call sites pass item-first per
-- that convention. Swapping would break downstream consumers.
--
-- The single-element list literal `[item]` lowers to a JS array
-- carrying the param ref (`[{var: "item"}]`); the canonical
-- opaque-repr pattern stores `List` with `repr: "array"`, so the
-- `concat` stdNative (which operates on `toArr(arr)`) accepts the
-- array form directly. Don't rewrite as `Cons item Nil` — that's
-- the pure-ADT view but `concat` expects the array repr.

@label "Append item"
@description "Append a single element to the end of a list. ∀a. a → List a → List a. Equivalent to `arr ++ [item]` in Haskell — the natural 1-element snoc that pairs with `concat` for chained appends. Phase 21.2 introduced this as the primitive that plays the role of TS's `arr.push(item)` in accumulator-threaded Fenel bodies (bare-array literals like `[item]` are rejected by Core IR at expression position, so a dedicated primitive is the self-hosting path)."
@tags "list" "append" "snoc"
appendItem : (item: a) -> (arr: List a) -> List a
appendItem item arr = concat arr [item]
